issue_context.body:
**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**
In our application, we have some external temperature sensors that get turned off based on the power state of an external component.

![Temp Sensor Power Domain](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/75284258/196256796-2eb5d133-ea92-4a8c-95c9-6e319d013493.png)

In this application, the power rail turns off/on asynchronously, and is not under the control of the Zephyr application.

The Zephyr application does monitor the power good signal as a GPIO input.

We're trying to model this using Zephyr's power domains.  We would like to create a power domain for the external "power rail".  If the power rail turns off, we want to notify the temperature sensor drivers, so the drivers don't attempt to communicate with an un-powered device.

The current power domain examples seem to be structured for a bottom-up management of the power domain.  Each device in the power domain calls `pm_device_runtime_get()` while the device is use.  If all devices in the domain are idle, the domain state is changed to suspended.

We're currently getting around this by using `pm_device_runtime_get/put()`  in our power rail driver:

```
power_rail_gpio_callback()
{
    if (power_good) {
        pm_device_runtime_get();
    } else {
        pm_device_runtime_put();
    }
}

power_rail_pm_action()
{
	switch (action) {
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_RESUME:
		pm_device_children_action_run(TURN_ON);
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_SUSPEND:
		pm_device_children_action_run(TURN_OFF);
}
```

**Describe the solution you'd like**

While using `pm_device_runtime_get/put()` at the power domain level is working for now, I don't think this is an intended use case.  If a temperature sensor in the domain makes a call to `pm_device_runtime_get/put()`, things will break due to the counting nature of get/put.

An API that let us directly set the power domain state is one idea we've considered.

I'm open to other ideas on how to model this.

comments:
My suggestion would be to model it as a power domain that fails to power up if it isn't already on, something like:
```
atomic_t power_up;

power_rail_gpio_callback()
{
    if (power_good) {
        pm_device_children_action_run(TURN_ON);
    } else {
        /* Possibility for weird behaviours here? Child devices don't transition through SUSPENDED? */
        pm_device_children_action_run(TURN_OFF);
    }
    power_up = power_good;
}

power_rail_pm_action()
{
	int rc = 0;
	switch (action) {
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON:
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF:
		return -ENOTSUP;
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_RESUME:
		if (!power_up) {
			return -EAGAIN;
		}
		break;
	default:
		break;
	}
	return rc;
}
```

review_comments:
If I understood correctly, `pm_action` shouldn't call `pd_gpio_monitor_update_status`, but check cached variable in data struct, and then return 0 or error. New `pm_action` should like this:
```
switch (action) {
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON:
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_OFF:
		return -ENOTSUP;
	case PM_DEVICE_ACTION_RESUME:
		if (!data->isPowered) {
		         rc = -EAGAIN;
                }
		break;
	default:
		break;
}
```
This would still enable support for `PM_DEVICE_RUNTIME`, and don't give any undesirable actions, only giving information about current power domain state.

Given the asynchronous nature of monitoring power domain, I don't think there is a perfect solution. I believe that 
zeroing `pm->usage`  every time power goes down is the best possible approach. The only possible side effect that I can see is that you mentioned the `pm_device_runtime_put` can be called the ref counter set to 0:

```
if (pm->usage == 0U) {
	LOG_WRN("Unbalanced suspend");
	ret = -EALREADY;
	goto unlock;
}
```

In that case, a warning message will be logged, and the user will be informed through the return value. Additionally, the drivers themselves should return that error if the power is interrupted.

What do you think?
